## Building Your Own Filter, Map, and Collect

Combinators take a collection and a rule, and produce a new collection. Let's build three core ones from scratch.

### Filter: Keep What Passes

`filter` keeps items that satisfy a condition. Since XIOM doesn't have closures yet, we'll hardcode the condition for each use:

```xiom
fn filter_positive(nums: &Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] {
  let result: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < nums.len() {
    let n = match nums.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    if n > 0 {
      result.push(n);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Map: Transform Every Item

`map` applies a transformation to each item. Again, hardcode the transform:

```xiom
fn double_all(nums: &Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] {
  let result: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < nums.len() {
    let n = match nums.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    result.push(n * 2);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Collect: Gather from Another Source

`collect` aggregates items from iteration into a new Vec:

```xiom
fn collect_even_up_to(max: Int) -> Vec[Int] {
  let result: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i <= max {
    if i % 2 == 0 {
      result.push(i);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Chaining Operations

The real power comes from chaining — the output of one becomes the input of the next:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let raw: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  raw.push(-5); raw.push(3); raw.push(-2); raw.push(8); raw.push(-1); raw.push(12);

  // Pipeline: filter positive -> double -> keep only > 20
  let step1 = filter_positive(&raw);
  let step2 = double_all(&step1);

  io.println("Final: " + string.int_to_str(step2.len()));
  return 0;
}
```

Each step is pure — it takes a Vec and returns a new Vec without modifying the original.

